1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,807
On most trains, each pair of wheels is locked to one solid steel axle, so both wheels always turn together.

2
00:00:07,613 --> 00:00:10,401
But on a bend, the outer wheel has farther to go.

3
00:00:11,226 --> 00:00:12,706
So why doesn't one of them skid?

4
00:00:13,912 --> 00:00:16,410
Take a bend that turns the track through a right angle.

5
00:00:17,152 --> 00:00:19,016
Here is the part you might not expect.

6
00:00:19,672 --> 00:00:22,219
Wide or tight, it makes no difference.

7
00:00:22,909 --> 00:00:26,364
The outer rail is about two point four meters longer either way.

8
00:00:27,036 --> 00:00:30,138
And a one-hundred-ton locomotive has nothing to fix that.

9
00:00:30,734 --> 00:00:36,044
No steering, no differential gears, the box that lets your car's outer wheel spin faster.

10
00:00:36,691 --> 00:00:42,143
Yet on a gentle curve, if you could watch the wheels, you would see that neither one really slips.

11
00:00:43,327 --> 00:00:47,194
The answer is in the shape of the wheel, and you've probably never noticed it.

12
00:00:47,828 --> 00:00:53,189
And that same shape explains the screech you hear on a tight bend, and why fast trains sway.

13
00:00:54,618 --> 00:00:56,111
So what steers it?

14
00:00:56,987 --> 00:00:59,979
The steel lips on the inside of each wheel, the flanges?

15
00:01:00,801 --> 00:01:02,801
That is what you would probably guess.

16
00:01:03,439 --> 00:01:04,753
But here is the surprise.

17
00:01:05,513 --> 00:01:09,613
On a gentle curve, the flanges do not need to touch the rail at all.

18
00:01:10,964 --> 00:01:14,755
Look closely at the tread, the part of the wheel that rolls on the rail.

19
00:01:15,447 --> 00:01:16,108
Do you see it?

20
00:01:17,127 --> 00:01:18,258
It is not flat.

21
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:23,645
It is tapered, close to a cone, so the wheel gets slightly bigger toward the flange.

22
00:01:24,467 --> 00:01:26,435
The slope is about one in twenty.

23
00:01:27,247 --> 00:01:31,461
If you move twenty millimeters across, its radius grows by one millimeter.

24
00:01:32,719 --> 00:01:35,003
Now watch the axle enter a curve.

25
00:01:35,795 --> 00:01:38,757
With both wheels the same size, it rolls straight on.

26
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:44,531
But the rails bend away beneath it, so it drifts a few millimeters toward the outside of the bend.

27
00:01:45,187 --> 00:01:46,751
And now you have the trick.

28
00:01:47,417 --> 00:01:54,555
That moves the outer wheel onto its fatter part, near the flange, and the inner wheel onto its thinner part, away from its flange.

29
00:01:55,312 --> 00:01:59,500
So the outer wheel rolls on a bigger circle, and the inner wheel on a smaller one.

30
00:02:00,201 --> 00:02:02,169
Same spin, different distance.

31
00:02:03,390 --> 00:02:07,888
It keeps drifting until the outer wheel is just big enough to cover the extra distance.

32
00:02:08,616 --> 00:02:13,036
Now neither wheel really slips, and the axle follows the curve by itself.

33
00:02:14,345 --> 00:02:15,756
How big is that difference?

34
00:02:16,608 --> 00:02:17,955
You might guess centimeters.

35
00:02:18,685 --> 00:02:23,599
Take the textbook version, two wheels and their axle, which engineers call a wheelset.

36
00:02:24,353 --> 00:02:30,137
If you shift it as far as it can go, its two wheels differ in radius by about seven tenths of a millimeter.

37
00:02:30,765 --> 00:02:33,066
About the thickness of the bank card in your wallet.

38
00:02:34,228 --> 00:02:38,591
So on a gentle curve, what you are looking at is the whole steering system.

39
00:02:39,214 --> 00:02:41,065
No gears, no levers.

40
00:02:41,549 --> 00:02:42,340
Just shape.

41
00:02:43,489 --> 00:02:45,071
It even works on straight track.

42
00:02:45,812 --> 00:02:48,206
Imagine you nudge the axle to one side.

43
00:02:48,837 --> 00:02:53,251
The wheel on that side gets bigger, so the axle steers itself back toward the middle.

44
00:02:54,606 --> 00:02:56,207
But shape has a limit.

45
00:02:57,046 --> 00:03:02,704
The textbook wheelset you just saw can only shift about seven millimeters before the flange meets the rail.

46
00:03:03,545 --> 00:03:08,379
If you put it on a curve with a radius of five hundred meters, it would need to shift fifteen.

47
00:03:09,226 --> 00:03:11,065
That is about twice what it has.

48
00:03:12,292 --> 00:03:20,297
So with a one in twenty cone, the shape alone only works on curves you would call gentle, about a kilometer in radius, or wider.

49
00:03:21,104 --> 00:03:23,872
Any tighter, and the flange starts to touch the rail.

50
00:03:25,074 --> 00:03:26,573
And on a really tight curve?

51
00:03:27,611 --> 00:03:32,632
The flange presses hard against the rail and drags the axle round, but at a slight angle.

52
00:03:33,417 --> 00:03:36,460
So the tread is scraped sideways across the top of the rail.

53
00:03:37,565 --> 00:03:39,448
And that is the screech you hear.

54
00:03:40,196 --> 00:03:42,157
You would think it is the flange rubbing, right?

55
00:03:43,123 --> 00:03:50,973
But most researchers say it is the tread, sliding sideways, sticking and slipping so fast that the whole wheel rings like a bell.

56
00:03:52,235 --> 00:03:54,976
On tight curves, the flange does the hard work.

57
00:03:55,703 --> 00:03:57,213
But how hard can you push it?

58
00:03:58,339 --> 00:04:01,960
More than a century ago, an engineer named Nadal worked that out.

59
00:04:02,810 --> 00:04:09,450
Once the sideways push gets roughly as big as the weight on that wheel, a typical flange can climb up and over the rail.

60
00:04:10,227 --> 00:04:12,409
That is one way a train comes off the track.

61
00:04:13,777 --> 00:04:15,266
And here is the twist.

62
00:04:16,070 --> 00:04:19,343
The cone that keeps your train centered also makes it sway.

63
00:04:20,567 --> 00:04:23,998
If you push the axle to one side, the cone steers it back.

64
00:04:24,750 --> 00:04:26,945
But does it stop in the middle, where you want it?

65
00:04:28,003 --> 00:04:28,527
No.

66
00:04:29,102 --> 00:04:31,444
It overshoots, and steers back again.

67
00:04:32,417 --> 00:04:37,230
If you watched from above, you would see the axle snake along the track in a smooth wave.

68
00:04:38,190 --> 00:04:41,261
Railway engineers call it hunting, and you can see why.

69
00:04:42,621 --> 00:04:46,076
George Stephenson knew about this sway about two hundred years ago.

70
00:04:46,839 --> 00:04:51,798
In eighteen eighty-three, an engineer called Klingel worked out how long each wave is.

71
00:04:52,559 --> 00:04:56,183
For the textbook wheelset you just saw, about seventeen meters.

72
00:04:56,973 --> 00:05:03,110
At a hundred and sixty kilometers an hour, if it kept going, that would be about two and a half sways every second.

73
00:05:04,347 --> 00:05:07,162
So why isn't your train always swaying like that?

74
00:05:08,175 --> 00:05:14,712
Because below a certain speed, called the critical speed, each sway is smaller than the last, and it dies away.

75
00:05:15,501 --> 00:05:20,283
Above it, each sway is bigger than the last, until the flanges hammer the rails.

76
00:05:21,472 --> 00:05:22,982
Fast trains have a fix.

77
00:05:23,407 --> 00:05:24,842
They use flatter cones.

78
00:05:25,519 --> 00:05:29,994
Japan's bullet trains used wheels with a slope of one in forty, half as steep.

79
00:05:30,793 --> 00:05:34,408
And holding each axle stiffly in its frame keeps it pointing straight.

80
00:05:35,156 --> 00:05:38,269
But that same stiffness makes it harder to turn into a curve.

81
00:05:39,551 --> 00:05:40,396
See the trade-off?

82
00:05:41,478 --> 00:05:45,254
A steeper cone, or a looser axle, takes tight curves better.

83
00:05:46,015 --> 00:05:49,684
A flatter cone, or a stiffer axle, stays calm at speed.

84
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:52,577
No single design is best at both.

85
00:05:53,966 --> 00:05:58,135
So the next time you hear that screech on a bend, you will know what it means.

86
00:05:58,844 --> 00:06:03,080
The cone has run out, and your train's steel wheels are sliding sideways.

87
00:06:04,186 --> 00:06:05,672
One solid axle.

88
00:06:06,253 --> 00:06:07,792
Two wheels locked together.

89
00:06:08,343 --> 00:06:10,552
And a gentle slope on every wheel.

90
00:06:11,143 --> 00:06:15,880
On gentle curves, the shape does the steering, and on tight ones, the flange helps.

91
00:06:16,562 --> 00:06:19,472
That is how a train steers with no steering at all.
